  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct approach is to configure permissions directly for <b>IAM roles, users, and groups accordingly</b>. In AWS Service Catalog, access to portfolios (and thus the products within them) is controlled by associating IAM users, groups, or roles to the portfolios. This approach provides granular, flexible access management suited to organizational needs.<br><br><i>Enable an AWS Organization and use Service Catalog Service Control Policies (SCPs) to limit user access</i> is incorrect. SCPs are used to set permission boundaries but do not directly control granular product access in Service Catalog; they apply broadly at the account or organizational level.<br><br><i>Utilize AWS Cognito User Pools to define authorization for individual products</i> is not accurate, as Cognito is typically used for application user authentication and not for managing permissions within AWS Service Catalog.<br><br><i>Set up Access Control List (ACL) policies via the Service Catalog console or AWS CLI to grant permissions</i> is misleading—Service Catalog uses IAM policies, not ACLs, to control resource permissions.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Always assign Service Catalog portfolio access through IAM to maintain precise control and take advantage of AWS's centralized permissions management.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Company ABC is leveraging AWS Service Catalog for organizing CloudFormation stacks across internal teams. Multiple portfolios have been established, each containing distinct products. The objective is to assign and restrict product access for different users or groups. For instance, only the DevOps team should be permitted to create, update, or remove a specific mission-critical payment service product. <br><br>In AWS Service Catalog, which method best achieves user access management?</p>",
  "original_oid": "6793da2d61969b416038a178",
